shell.html: a control page for what this device is holding

The shell is deliberately hard to dislodge — `sw.js` is cache-first over
`FALLBACK_SHELL` and never consults the network on a hit, which is the possession
guarantee working as designed. But the aggression had no lever pointed at the
**holder**: short of `destruct.html` there was no way to ask the cache to go
forward, and two signals the worker already records went nowhere.

- `precache()` counts its failures and returns them; `install()` threw them into
  `console.warn`, so "the shell is incomplete offline" was invisible at exactly
  the moment nobody can debug it.
- `checkFirmware()` / `adoptOffer()` write the pin's `rejected` record on a
  refusal. Nothing has ever read it back — a possessed-origin refusal was silent.

Both surface on `/shell.html` now, alongside what served the page and what the
cache actually holds.

## The two levers

**Refresh the shell** re-fetches every shell path in place. No unregister, no pin
change, no trove access; worst case you re-download the shell. This is what "roll
forward" means while firmware pinning is dormant — which is the state production
is actually in, since no `firmware.json` is deployed.

**Check for a signed update** renders the decision the worker was already
replying with and nothing displayed.

`shellRefresh()` **refuses under a live pin** rather than walking around it. An
unsigned byte-for-byte overwrite from the same origin would defeat exactly what
the pin promises, so under a pin the only way forward is a signed manifest — and
the page names the refusal instead of failing quietly.

## Why this page is in the shell and the hatch is not

`/shell.html` is in `FALLBACK_SHELL`; `/destruct.html` deliberately is not. The
distinction is the verb. The hatch stays off the shell so you cannot destroy your
only copy while the origin is dead. This page only **looks** and **refreshes**,
and looking is precisely what you need when the origin is gone.

It never refuses to show you something. Every probe renders independently, the
page falls back to reading the Cache API and IndexedDB directly when the worker
is silent, and failures render as text. There is no path here that ends in a
blank screen.

## Tests

`scripts/sw.test.mjs` makes three of `sw.js`'s prose invariants executable:

1. The **VERSION / FALLBACK_SHELL bump rule**, via a pinned digest literal.
   Editing the list without bumping the key strands every existing install on the
   old copy where no edge purge can reach them. The digest is a literal on
   purpose — computing it from the list the test just parsed would compare a
   value to itself and pass forever.
2. **Every shell path exists.** A typo'd entry cannot be precached, so the
   offline shell is quietly incomplete.
3. **The verb split** — `shell.html` in, `destruct.html` out.

Verified in Chromium: v7 installs, the page reports 49/49 held, the refresh lever
round-trips, and the page still boots and renders correctly **with the origin
killed**. `119/119` suites pass.

## Also in here

`docs/coring.md` — a shaping note capturing the redaction/disclosure layer this
work sits under. Coring proves *possession of the rest*, not derivation, so it
needs no zero-knowledge machinery; the format's grid picks the redaction size
rather than the author; chunk chaining asserts edges and count without content.
Companion to `data-pile/CONTRACT.md` Layer 3, which it does not replace.

## Deliberately not in here

- The **held / rolling two-slot split** and the rolling-by-default, latch-on-
  intent policy.
- `sw.js`'s `activate()` deleting the previous shell on a VERSION bump before the
  replacement is proven whole. That is a live lockout path: kill the network
  mid-install and the old floor is already gone. This page makes it observable,
  which is the right order, but the fix changes the possession guarantee's
  default and wants its own note in `origin.md`.

